    {
      "id": "send-for-sallow",
      "source": "start",
      "target": "face-to-face",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "Master Sallow steps in",
      "data": {
        "condition": "Only while Master Sallow is free this morning (not in a cell, not fled). The player asks for him to be made to explain himself in front of the King ('call him in', 'ask him where the grain went'), or says his name where the gallery can hear; or the King sends for him of his own accord, once he has heard a case against an officer of the Crown (he likes to hear everyone); or Sallow comes in by himself, to have the King's ear before the investigator has finished. No dice: the King grants it at once ('Garran, ask Master Sallow to step in'). The outer limit: if he's free and hasn't been in by the time the Marshal calls fifteen minutes, he knocks and comes in to pay his respects before the procession, and to offer to look into any irregularities himself, after the Assembly.",
        "phase": "cue",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "caught-out",
      "source": "face-to-face",
      "target": "sallow-slips",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "It came out of his own mouth",
      "data": {
        "condition": "",
        "phase": "outcome",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "time-presses",
      "source": "start",
      "target": "reckoning",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "What's to be done",
      "data": {
        "condition": "The player has told the King what they found (who killed Prout, and why) and shown or named what proof they have, in their own way, and asks him for something: to act today, to arrest or charge Sallow, to set the record straight, to keep a promise. Or they've been talking a good while (about four exchanges) without coming to the point. If Sallow is in the room, he has his say first (his own route). The outer limit: the Marshal calls five minutes, and the King asks, gently, what he is to do.",
        "phase": "cue",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sallow-answered",
      "source": "face-to-face",
      "target": "reckoning",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "Sallow has had his say",
      "data": {
        "condition": "Sallow has been in the room a good while (about four exchanges) and answered for himself, giving himself away or not, or the player has finished with him: the Marshal calls the time, and the King turns back to the investigator to ask what he is to do.",
        "phase": "cue",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "king-acts",
      "source": "what-moves-him",
      "target": "justice",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "The King's word",
      "data": {
        "condition": "The King gives his word to act today, before the procession: Master Sallow taken by the palace guard (or held where he is, if Kell took him in the night) for the Assizes, and the record to say murder. He does it because the player has given him what moves him: something he can hold (Prout's page or the true book in his hands, Hobb's sworn word, Kell's sworn record pointing at Sallow, Sallow giving himself away in his hearing, or Sallow already in a cell on Kell's sworn arrest) and someone standing behind it in front of him (Kell, or the investigator themselves). No dice: given that, he puts his cup down and says 'Yes.' Then, to the Marshal: 'Garran, see to it.'",
        "phase": "outcome",
        "requires": [
          "what-moves-him"
        ],
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "press-the-king",
      "source": "reckoning",
      "target": "justice",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "Pressed to act",
      "data": {
        "condition": "The player presses the King to act today on less than something he can hold with someone standing behind it in front of him (no page or book in his hands, no sworn word, no slip of Sallow's in his hearing, nobody to stand behind it), where it could go either way. Success: he puts his cup down, looks at the investigator for a long moment, and says 'Yes. Garran, see to it.' Failure leaves the scene where it is, and costs: he pats the investigator's arm and reaches for 'after the Assembly' without quite getting there, the Marshal asks 'On whose word, Majesty?' of nobody in particular and calls the minutes, fewer now, and asking again won't move him on its own: he wants something he can hold.",
        "phase": "outcome",
        "requires": [
          "what-moves-him"
        ],
        "skill": "persuasion",
        "dc": 15
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "put-off",
      "source": "reckoning",
      "target": "after-the-assembly",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "After the Assembly",
      "data": {
        "condition": "The King has put it off for good: the ninth bell has rung and he has gone down to the procession with it to be looked into after the Assembly, or the player has accepted that and let it rest. (The King reaching for 'after the Assembly', or saying it while the player is still pressing or still has something to put to him, isn't this: that's his dilemma, not his decision.)",
        "phase": "outcome",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "thrown-away",
      "source": "start",
      "target": "shown-out",
      "type": "smoothstep",
      "label": "Last resort",
      "data": {
        "condition": "Last resort only: the player has made a scene the King can't overlook (drawn steel or struck a blow in the dressing room, at Master Sallow or anyone; threatened the King; shouted it from the windows to the delegations or the crowd), or has walked out on the King before telling him what they found. Not for a hard question, a rude word or an accusation: those the King hears out.",
        "phase": "outcome",
        "skill": "",
        "dc": 12
      }
